    sol: "It's risky because design/product decisions get justified by appeal to an imagined user whose goals and behaviors may not reflect anyone real, effectively laundering the founder's personal assumptions as if they were validated research — and nobody catches the gap until a real launch reveals the mismatch. Fix it by conducting a small round of actual user interviews (even 6-8), grounding the persona's goals/pain points/behaviors in real recurring patterns from that data, and explicitly retiring or revising the assumption-based version.",
    exp: "A persona's value comes entirely from being evidence-based; an assumption-based one is worse than no persona at all, since it creates false confidence.",
