    q: "Critique this login form's usability: placeholder-only field labels, low-contrast gray placeholder text, no visible error states, and a submit button the same gray as the page background.",
    sol: "Placeholder-only labels disappear the moment the user starts typing, losing the field's context exactly when it's most needed — add persistent labels above or beside each field. Low-contrast gray text likely fails WCAG's 4.5:1 ratio — darken it or increase weight. No visible error states means failed logins give no actionable feedback — add an inline, specific error message. A submit button that blends into the background has no figure-ground separation and no clear affordance — increase contrast and add a visual cue that signals it's clickable.",
    exp: "Each issue maps to a distinct, known principle (persistent labeling, contrast, error visibility, affordance) rather than a vague 'this looks off.'",
